.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::Util 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::Util
.Nd deadlines, polls and readable sizes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::Util;

my $result = Fugu::Util::bounded(10, sub { $client->connect });

Fugu::Util::wait_until(60, 0.5, sub { -S $socket })
    or die "the daemon never came up\en";

printf "%s of cache\en", Fugu::Util::format_size($bytes);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
holds three small helpers that otherwise get a copy in every
subsystem: a wall-clock guard, a bounded poll, and a byte count that a
person can read.
.Pp
The three are plain functions, not methods.
They keep no state and they never log.
.Ss bounded
.Fn bounded "$seconds" "$code"
runs
.Fa $code
under a hard wall-clock deadline.
A blocked call inside
.Fa $code
cannot stall the caller for longer than
.Fa $seconds .
.Pp
The guard is an
.Xr alarm 3 ,
so it interrupts a blocking system call that a poll loop cannot reach.
That is why it exists next to
.Fn wait_until ,
which cannot do that.
.Pp
A die inside
.Fa $code
propagates, with the alarm already cleared.
Thus a real failure keeps its own message and is not reported as a
timeout.
.Ss wait_until
.Fn wait_until "$timeout" "$interval" "$code"
calls
.Fa $code
until it returns true, or until
.Fa $timeout
seconds pass.
.Fa $interval
is the pause between calls; its default is a quarter of a second.
.Pp
.Fa $code
runs at least once, even with a timeout of 0.
A caller that asks
.Dq is it ready
always gets an answer about now.
.Pp
The loop also stops when a signal arrives, through
.Xr Fugu::Signal 3p .
A poll loop must not outlive the interrupt that told the program to
stop.
.Ss format_size
.Fn format_size "$bytes"
returns the byte count in the shortest unit that keeps it under 1024.
The units are B, K, M, G and T.
A count below 1024 has no decimal; the others have one.
.Pp
A count of
.Dv undef
reads as a question mark.
A size that nobody could measure is not a size of zero.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn bounded
returns what
.Fa $code
returned.
It returns
.Dv undef
when the deadline elapsed.
.Pp
.Fn wait_until
returns the first true value that
.Fa $code
gave.
It returns
.Dv undef
on the timeout, and
.Dv undef
when a signal arrived.
.Pp
.Fn format_size
returns a short string.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example bounds a guest interaction that can block for ever:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $out = Fugu::Util::bounded(15, sub {
    return $ssh->run_command('sync; sync; sync');
});
warn "the guest did not answer\en" unless defined $out;
.Ed
.Pp
This example waits for a port to open, and stops early on a
.Dv SIGINT :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Fugu::Util::wait_until(120, 1, sub {
    IO::Socket::INET->new(PeerAddr => '127.0.0.1', PeerPort => $port);
}) or die "port $port never opened\en";
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn bounded
lets a die from
.Fa $code
through unchanged.
It has no error of its own: a deadline is a return value, not an
exception.
.Pp
.Fn wait_until
and
.Fn format_size
do not die.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr alarm 3 ,
.Xr Fugu::Process 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Signal 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn bounded
uses
.Xr alarm 3 ,
which is process-global and has one pending deadline.
A nested
.Fn bounded
replaces the outer deadline and does not restore it.
Do not nest the two.
.Pp
An alarm interrupts a system call in progress.
A caller whose
.Fa $code
writes to a file can therefore leave a partial write behind.
Bound a read, a connect or a wait, and not a write that must be whole.
.Pp
.Fn wait_until
cannot interrupt a
.Fa $code
that blocks.
The timeout bounds the loop, not one call inside it.
Use
.Fn bounded
for that.
